    "turn_text": "Welcome to Kitty of Liberty. Congressman. Good to see you. Good to see you, Matt. This is the most spontaneous kibbe on liberty ever because I saw you on a live stream on this hearing that Chip Roy hearing that Chip Roy put together with some of my favorite epidemiologists and I texted you and like, Hey, let's do something. So. Yeah, I'm in a round table, congressional round table being live streamed, and I'm texting with you to come over here and do this. I'm like, sure, let's do it. So it was Jay Bottacharya, who's been on the show a couple times, Martin Koldorf, co-signer of the Great Barrington Declaration, and Scott Atlas, who was briefly President Trump's whatever virus advisor, COVID advisor. For as long as he could stand being in the room with the Covidiots. Yeah, the Covidiots. What was your takeaway from that hearing? Did you learn anything new? What was the point? What is Chip trying to accomplish with that? I shouldn't call it a hearing. Yeah, what Chip Roy organized it. It's sad, but we had to have a block from the capital. Because, you know, we're not in the majority yet. And even if we are, they may not want to have this kind of hearing. And Chip Roy's goal was to make sure we don't just forget what's happened. Because we just had an election where a lot of people who did these shutdowns and lockdowns and mandates have I've essentially been forgiven. I mean, the electorate gave them a pass. Some of them a pass. And so Chip wanted to make sure we got the scientists who were right all along from the very beginning on record. And we still have vaccine mandates. There's still vaccine mandates at universities. There's the government vaccine mandate to come into this country legally. You have to have the vaccine. You know, there's the military vaccine mandate. There's the CMS vaccine mandate, which is the healthcare vaccine mandate. And what they pointed out, I don't know if this was, which of the three of the epidemiologists and public health experts, which they really are the experts, pointed this out is the medical vaccine mandate caused a lot of nurses to leave and create a shortage of healthcare workers during a pandemic. So that was one of the, and by the way, they were the ones who had the highest, they were the cohort with the highest degree of cerebral prevalence, which means Most of them already got COVID because they were the first to interact with COVID patients. So they had natural immunity. So it didn't make scientific sense. And it made the healthcare system worse. And I mean, that's just kind of fun. Fun fact about that specific thing where the government basically forced hospitals to fire these frontline healthcare workers. There is a recently released national security memorandum from the Biden administration, which if you haven't read it, you need to read it. And I'll talk about it a little bit later, because it really just doubles down on the entire pandemic industrial complex paradigm from testing to dangerous experimentation with viruses. But one of the things they've committed to doing is that the federal government is going to fund the training of new healthcare workers because it turns out there's a shortage. And I feel like this is just like a Ronald Reagan adage here somewhere. It's like they for a while they dropped the high school diploma requirement for recruiting into the military because they had damaged recruiting into the military with the vaccine mandates and a lot of young individuals who had a lot of common sense decided not to go in. Yeah. I guess the surprise that I had there was among these three doctors, one of their biggest concerns or one of the biggest mistakes made during this whole COVID response was what I've been calling out all along, which is the complete denial of natural immunity. which has been with us for thousands of years, they understood the natural immunity. And that's been the biggest. It's like one of the scientists there said it would be like NASA, the CDC, ignoring natural immunity, would be like NASA saying that the Earth is not round, that it's flat. Before I forget about it, do you have legislation to protect the military members who have decided for whatever reason to not get vaccinated? Where is that? H.R. 3860. I have, I think, 91 co-sponsors. There should be 434 co-sponsors. At least there should be the entire Republican conference, but there's not. Are they all Republican so far? They're all Republicans so far. I'm concerned that not enough of them on the Armed Services Committee where that bill's been referred. You know, they always like, Oh, well, we'll defer to the experts, the military experts. It's basically it defunds not just the vaccine, COVID vaccine mandate for the military, but any disparate treatment of members of the military. Like you can't force them to take substandard housing like They have, you can't deny them training, you can't make them wear masks, all of the stupid things that they were doing before there was a vaccine mandate. And by the way, the reason I introduced the bill and it only covered the military is I introduced it before there were any mandates. And it was the only one I could anticipate them believing they had the authority to do. You know, the reason I didn't do the OSHA vaccine mandate is that at the time, OSHA was going to stand up for workers who had been forced to take the vaccine. Like, OSHA was saying that employees have to report this as a work-related entry if you have adverse effect from the vaccine. And so I couldn't possibly anticipate OSHA would go from protecting workers to like mandating- So even Massey with his libertarian tenfold heart. You're like, they would never go that far. It's all my tin foil hat could hold was to imagine that Biden would do a vaccine mandate for the military. Now, the other thing that I never really anticipated is that he would not know as much about science or medicine as George Washington. Yeah. Because that was the precedent. You know, they always say, Oh, well, George Washington made people get the smallpox inoculation. We'll go read his orders. in but in two places i can find his orders it said all the all who've not had it all who've not had smallpox which included and by the way george washington had smallpox as an adolescent so he never took it either he understood natural unity and uh... joe biden does well in biden's defense at least there was no executive order on mandatory leaching so so there has been progress there has been progress but even leaching made more sense than, you know, what's gone on. And by the way, now they're sort of kind of acknowledging that, you know, some of these off-patent, generic things like Ivermectrin might have worked a couple years late. Yeah, I want to dig into that, and we should frame this conversation because you mentioned the election happened a couple days ago, and we don't actually... Well, it's still happening. It's still happening. Apparently in Arizona, this could go on for many months now. So most news sources are projecting that the House will flip to Republican, but by fairly narrow majorities. Nobody is yet projecting the Senate, so it's still possible the Republicans might take the Senate, but we don't know that, so in the context of that, I have to observe going back. I'm shocked. I mean, 90-plus co-sponsors is great for that legislation, but that should be a slam dunk for Republicans in an election year fighting against the encroachments of the Biden administration that was clearly the grassroots fire that they needed to tap into. And they didn't. Maybe there's a reason why. They didn't, because I pulled this in my Republican primary, and it was number six or seven on the list of concerns. It was below inflation, below jobs, below the economy. Because it only affects some people. Because people go about, they've kind of ignored the government at this point, and they've moved on. Okay, for instance, the by-valent booster that Pfizer announced charges, it's not just by-valent, it's by-dollar. They've doubled the price of it. Only 7% of Americans have received that booster. And it's literally the only one responsive to the virus that's circulating right now. So I think Americans have just, they figured out how to go on with life and just ignore Rachel Walensky. He's just recovered from a three week bout of COVID after five shots and packs COVID. She's thankful that it was only three weeks. They're like robots when they say that. I'm sad to announce that I've contracted COVID yet again. But thank goodness I got boosted three times. Yeah, just change in. We're in is now five. Yeah, instead of three. And the ones that say four, you know, the ones who've gotten five boosts are probably saying, well, there's your problem. You didn't have five. You didn't get the biovalent booster that was tested on eight mice. So So this is something in the here. Sorry to interrupt, but 7% of Americans are buying it at this point, and it's free, right? So I guess that makes 93% of us, like vaccine deniers, anti-vaxxers, because that was the definition of anti-vaxxer, if you didn't take COVID vaccine. One of the, I mean, you mentioned Ivermectin, And one of those sort of glaring questions that remains to be answered is, why was natural immunity not only dismissed but attacked as a conspiracy theory? Why were treatments that were not vaccines, treatments with a proven track record, ignored at the expense of just monolithically, just like, we're gonna do the vaccine, you gotta get Vaccine age, you got to get boosted. And I have a fun fact. This week, my cat got monoclonal antibodies for the arthritis in his back legs. I could not get monoclonal antibodies for my wife when she got COVID last year, because Biden took it off the market. So there's a free market in medicine for my cat, who's doing much better, by the way. I'm glad you asked. Maybe it'll, yeah. Don't don't don't don't don't don't don't spew your hateful thoughts about cats. I'm not hateful. Just The world doesn't need any more cats, but the ones we have are fine. I'd be like well works happy to know that you're not gonna send them to the kitty camp No, no, I love animals by the way love animals. Yeah, just not cats not cats. It's not among the list But, what were we talking about? Monoclonal antibodies. Yeah, like, and we will clip that part. Which you did. You did that on the movie that we made off the grid with Thomas Massey. You didn't put it in the movie, but you did an outtake of me talking about cats and it made my mom mad. She saw it. Well, your mom understands more about the world than you do. I know she's always right. Yeah. But no, they're not taking them by valence. So that makes 93% of his anti-vaxxers, I guess. But people have moved on. So I think that's why you didn't see an appeal in the elections to take out the perpetrators of COVID tyranny. Tyrants is the word I was looking for. The Tyrants got reelected. I'm sorry. So let's parse that a little bit. And again, we only know certain things about the election, but I do think I see a pattern where certain Republicans performed quite well having fought lockdown. Yeah, the exception being, and was Florida. So here's the irony. Okay. the pundits looked at the early votes in florida and the turnout and republicans were turning out great and um florida's one of those states that does report their results on election night they can count they can count it's math is hard but is this a simple addition you know uh my wife's like they should somebody should have taught him about putting that fifth slash sideways through the marks it makes it a lot easier to count but they've learned how to do that in Florida. So one of the reasons the prediction of the big red wave was so far off is they were looking at Florida at the data in Florida and they were looking at Ron DeSantis' performance. Ron DeSantis had a big red wave. I mean he left the other in the dust and Rubio did fine and a lot of people were buoyed into office of Republicans in Florida because of that. But you can't extrapolate Ron DeSantis as a leader of the Republican Party in Florida to the rest of America. There may have been a big red wave, for instance, Mike DeWine in Ohio, one of the worst Republican lockdown governors. Uh, I think he's actually older than Biden and about at the same mental capacity at this point. I, you know, I don't care. All I care about is like he had walked down. He was awful. He was worse than some Democrats. And so, you know, that, I think that tempered enthusiasm could have been better. Yeah. So, and I'm cherry picking here because I've frankly been struggling to find the narrative in this election because I was absolutely someone that thought having been a veteran of the Tea Party wave of 2010 that Republicans would win simply because the Democrats owned lockdowns, they owned trillions and trillions in deficit spending that has caused all this inflation and all that stuff. I was wrong. Thank you for joining me today on Kibion Liberty and for being part of our fiercely independent audience. Every week my organization Free the People partners with Blaze TV to bring you this show. My guest brings smart perspectives on everything from current events to timeless philosophical debates. If you like what you hear, go to freethepeople.org slash KOL and support Kibion Liberty so we can continue to produce these honest conversations with interesting people. Now let's get back to it. Let me take issue with that. Maybe the voters are smarter than we give them credit for. Maybe they know what you and I know, which is it was Republican. Nancy Pelosi was in the house, but Donald Trump was the president. When the 15 days to slow the spread became at least nine months under him, the Fauci and Birx weren't fired. And we passed $2.2 trillion. And the president was the cheerleader of that and the vaccines. uh, you know, accelerating their, their production and the FDA approval. So like, why should you give Republicans credit when you've got Mike DeWine in Ohio being worse than most Democrats when you get, you know, you're screaming at me for opposing the $2 trillion bill. Cause I said at the time it would cause inflation and shortages. Like maybe the voters know that whichever party you put in there, they both voted for that. Yeah, your theory is more credible than mine because I was thinking that they had short memories and that since Biden and the Democrats have controlled things and they pretty much doubled down on everything that bad that Trump did. But it turns out that I want to retell this story. I love you telling this story because I think I think you this show is something that you did first Right after you became the most hated man in Washington DC in March of 2020 March 27th 2020. Yeah, you know that will live in infinity I call it the singularity Yeah, the world collapsed on me like the top three stories on drudge report were me yeah, and and Geraldo Rivera was talking about how bad I was and Trump screaming at me on the phone. Nancy Pelosi goes on MSNBC and calls me a dangerous nuisance. Like people coming up to me saying, you know, people are going to die because of what I'm doing. Like, it was not a good day for me. I'll remember March 27, 2020. By the way, the 11th day to slow spread. The irony of the argument of the congressional leadership that it was too dangerous to gather in person suggests to me that even Nancy Pelosi acknowledged that congressional members of Congress are not essential workers. Is that possible? Do you want me to argue with that? Like if she was saying that, I might be agreeing with her. But no, ostensibly we are essential. No law can be passed without us. No money can be spent without us. And if you're going to respond to an emergency, we should be present and debating it, not eating ice cream out of $20,000 refrigerators and calling in the vote. And I said at the time, if farmers and truckers and nurses and grocery store workers are working, then by galley gets your butts to congress and i was that's what i was hated for yeah asking not asking requiring because the constitution requires it requiring people to show up because i was alone dissenting boys and i had members of my own party who are going to be in leadership now screaming at me on the phone to i know uh... for doing it so like literally that the republican president and the republican leaders were your worst enemies in that fight Nearly everybody except for you. You invited me on that day. I'm cool. I don't have any friends. They were collapsed on me. I think it was maybe the next day or something. Because I had been sleeping in my car for three days. I didn't know when they were going to call the motion. I'm literally eating pretzels with peanut butter in the middle. In the back of my expedition, it camped out in the parking lot of Congress. waiting for them like by the way if there's one perfect food its pretzels was peanut butter like I it's got the salt it's got the carbs it's got the protein it's probably that margaritas which is what I had in the back of my expedition is this like the massive food pyramid now more truth comes out I had medical margaritas I don't drink recreationally yeah I understand So to remind people, all that nonsense, you had the audacity to ask Congress to vote on the largest spending bill in history. And the entire DC establishment came down on you like a ton of bricks. They came down on me like a ton of bricks and I pulled it a couple weeks ago in my district. Only polled Republicans, because I was in a primary fight in my life since the first serious challengers at well-funded since I'd been elected. And so it wasn't just people hating me. I can get over that pretty easily. But I was about to be unelected. I felt if I did that. But I polled it. And I even tried what messaging would work best. Among Republicans, I said, are you in favor of the lockdowns? And 79, I'm sorry, are you in favor of like emergency spending? I didn't call it lockdowns. I didn't pull the lockdowns. Are you in favor of the emergency spending, the bailout? And 79% of Republicans in my congressional district supported the bailout. Yeah. And if I called it stimulus, so at half the poll, I'd say, were you in favor of the stimulus? It went from 79% to 81% popular among Republicans. So I wasn't just out of step with Washington DC. We always like to blame the swamp, but the swamp responds to what people, the electorate wants or what they're polling. And at the time the polling was against me too, in my own district. But ironically, coincidentally, whatever, in spite of that six or eight weeks later, I got 81% of the vote in my district. Even though 81% supported the bill, I was the only person in the world opposing. Well, let's, let's talk about that. I don't, I'm not sure we've ever talked about it in great detail, but the threat to you was very much inspired by Trump. So Trump wanted this bill to pass. He wanted it to pass immediately. He called you up. He took your head off. Did he actually endorse your primary opponent? No, he, he called me a third rate grandstander on Twitter. And he did say I should be thrown out of the GOP that the Kentucky Party should throw me out of the Republican Party. And then the third tweet was... So kind of a non-endorsement. The third tweet said, and at this point I imagine he's out of ideas. And he turns to somebody and say, what would hurt him the most? And they say, well, he's the chairman of the Second Amendment caucus. So he tweets that I'm bad on guns. Like, that was the third tweet that nobody remembers. But I remember it. The guy that said, grab them and then have a trial. Yeah. Yeah. We kind of made up since then because then now this primary ahead, he called up and endorsed me a few days before my election. So we came around. Yeah. But you survived. So would you say, you know, as unpopular as standing on principle in the middle of a panic was, does it pay political dividends for you in your reelection this last week? Yes. I mean, the media in my district, they were wondering, well, why aren't you campaigning more? Like, we noticed you're not doing a lot of campaigning. We're not seeing your TV ads. Where's the mail in the mailboxes? And my response was, look, everybody right now is trying to brand themselves as being against inflation. That is my brand. I don't need to brand myself. Everybody knows that I opposed not just the two trillion, but the four trillion that followed, all six trillion, not just a little bit, not just with a vote, but vocally, whether it was Biden or Trump, I was opposed to it. So I didn't need to campaign against the number one issue, I had already been the only person on the planet trying to stop the number one issue from happening. Those little payouts to voters in the midst of their panic was the cheese and the trap. If you could, yeah, the 12th, that's what I said, that bill, by the way, I tweeted this, I think the day I came on your show, I said that the 2.2 trillion dollar stimulus bill is the biggest transfer of wealth. from the middle class to the Wall Street bankers and Wall Street in the history of mankind and the $1,200 is the cheese in the trap. And it was the cheese in the trap. Now they added a little more cheese. They did another $600 piece of cheese. and a $1,400 piece of cheese. There's always a piece of cheese. But all that cheese adds up to maybe $33,000. And if you got a lot of kids and whatever, maybe you got more than that. But it doesn't add up to what you lost in inflation just this year. And the inflation is permanent. So they announced, I think in August, when the government admits that inflation is 8.3%, it's probably a lot higher than that. But 8.3%, if you take 1 over 12, it's 8.3%. Well, there's 12 months in a year. Everybody lost one month of pay this year due to inflation. So the cheese, like you had the cheese, the cheese is gone, now you're in the sticky trap. And it's not just that you lost 8.3% this year, that's permanent. And you may have gotten a raise, but they're going to outpace it. And what has been the government solution to, by the way, they didn't overstimulate the economy. We're not living in a raging economy right now. The shortages aren't caused by massive demand. The shortages are caused by people working at home or just fell out of the workforce. or things that didn't happen two years ago that I may have mentioned this on your show too at some point that fruit trees weren't planted two summers ago. I've got fruit trees on my farm. It takes three years to get fruit from a tree. Like the mistakes that were made two years ago, it takes three years to breed a dairy cow, it grows up, it has a baby and then it gets milk, okay, that's three years. I was pointing out that summer as things stayed shut down, that everything you're doing is gonna have a consequence that goes at least three years from now. So we've got not a raging economy, we've got raging inflation. What they normally do to curb raging inflation, the Federal Reserve, is they raise interest rates. Well, that's great if you've got a raging economy. They say, well, we've got to cool off the economy. We don't have a raging economy, but it's the only lever they've got right now. By the way, this is six weeks after they pass the Inflation Reduction Act. I'm pretty sure the Fed ain't buying the name of that bill, because if they believed it was going to reduce inflation, they wouldn't have increased interest rates by quicker in a shorter period of time by more than they've ever done in history. They are throwing so many wet blankets, not on a fire, not on a raging economy, but on a ho-hum economy. And so now the people, the people, you know, we all took the cheese, okay, there was no choice, you know, because the government has forced it. But now that you've had the cheese, not only do you have one-twelfth of your pay gone this year, You are stuck in your house, because if you've got a mortgage at 3%, and you go, you say, oh, I like that house down the street. It's got a bigger yard and a place for our kids to play. And it's closer to work. Let's move there. Well, guess what? Look at your mortgage payment. It may be the same size house and everything is the same. It's just in a better location. You can buy a third of the house now because the cheese was in the trap. The twelve hundred, six hundred, fourteen hundred dollars has now led to the Fed raising interest rates. And if you have to borrow capital to, you know, build a house or whatever you're paying for it. It's like the laws of economics don't care about politics. They don't care. They don't care at all. But people care. Yeah. And we got inflation and we got all that and maybe the red wave should have been bigger. But one thing I do want to say, you haven't asked me this question and I know I'm assuming we've got a majority. I keep getting asked, is it going to be hard to govern? Okay, so whether the Republicans have the majority or the Democrats do and Okay, this is where you're gonna have to like put a subtitle on the video if I'm wrong about this, but I'm hoping you make a horrible that we can embarrass you Okay, either party's gonna get a majority by just a few seats. Yeah, and I've been asked does that make it harder to govern? And I am just, I am just delighted at the prospect of having just a few seat majority. because they need to consider if the speaker, let's say it's going to be Republican, decides to pass a bill and the speaker has 218 votes and the minority party has 217 and the minority party desperately wants to make the majority party look bad so there's no coming across the aisle. The 208, every member of the Republican conference, their opinion has to be considered in order for that bill to pass. One of the worst things that could happen, and I've seen this in state legislatures and red states, is you get a super majority. And then the lobbyists come in, the unions come in, everybody, bankers come in and the leadership and leadership says, well I can solve this problem and I only need 50% plus one and I can marginalize these 30 people who understand that this is going to be a bad bill. I think you could get a really good bill if every member of one party's point of view was considered, which is where we're headed. If you made it this far into the show, it means I must be doing something right. Kibion Liberty is just one of the amazing products we created for the people. We tell emotionally compelling stories and produce educational videos for the Liberty curious. Our award-winning documentaries personalize all things liberty, independence, creativity, hard work, integrity, and perseverance. After the show, check out our work at freethepeople.org. And if you like what you see, donate to support what we do. That's freethepeople.org. Now back to the show. I thought you were going to suggest that with 218 Republicans, there's actually 217 Republicans and then the party of Massey. Well, there's been the party... You could be the kingmaker. You could be the guy. There's been the party of... Somebody asked me, is it hard to govern when you only have a one-seat majority? And I said, I don't think so. Go ask Joe Manchin. Yeah. Like Joe Manchin's been in the catbird seat, not Chuck Schumer. Joe Manchin, people know more about Joe Manchin than they do about Chuck Schumer because he's actually somebody who's for a while anyway, stood on principle. and but everybody has the chance to be a Joe Manchin like there could have been other Democrats right that took Joe Manchin's position but they chose not to they're like wait we're just going along to get along and in the house I think there'll be a similar scenario where You'll have 180 who decide to go along to get along who have 20 people. I call it prom court, the leadership. You've got people running for speaker right now. My phone is blowing up while I'm talking to you. It's buzzing with area codes from Texas and Indiana. Everybody's running. for speaker, majority leader, uh, whip, all the way down to executive vice deputy prom queen of the GOP conference. Yes. Like executive vice deputy prom queen of the GOP conference. Like that's a position that people back home don't even know exists, but you know, people are running for things like that. And that's what's happening right now. But it's like you used to have to have one of those titles to even get a seat at the table in what might be happening behind closed doors. Now everybody's executive vice deputy prom queen of the GOP. And you're like you are particularly bulletproof because the machine has never helped you. Is that an overstatement? I don't feel obligated to the machine. But here's the other benefit of a slim majority. If I may, opine. on the benefits of a slim majority. A lot of the people in these tough districts that were going to win were going to have to march the line. They were going to be the ones that if the NRCC spent $5 million or $10 million or $2 million on your race and got you through by the skin of your teeth, uh... they are they on you they for the most part i've seen exceptions rodd blum was an exception that got voted against john bainer like you know the day he got elected after this and i like five million dollars getting elected and i will uh... so there are a few exceptions there are not many uh... but that's another benefit of slim majority like the the party apparatus doesn't have those people in swing districts that they could count on to marginalize the conservatives in the safe districts. I want to go back to the politics of lockdowns and political accountability and the benefits of running on principle because I'm clinging to this idea that Republicans can win when they stand for something that's different than what the Democrats are proposing. And it wasn't just Ron